      "id": "b-26f00h",
      "type": "cover",
      "kicker": "Press Trust of India",
      "title": "The '26' factor",
      "standfirst": "Nepal’s flash floods are the latest addition to a grim history of disasters that struck on the 26th — twenty-two of them, across ninety-six years.",
      "author": "PTI, New Delhi",
      "date": "31 August 2026",
      "media": {
        "kind": "image",
        "src": "a-26f008",
        "alt": "The Langtang valley in Rasuwa district, Nepal."
      },
      "scrim": 0.82,
      "focal": "50% 45%",
      "cue": true,
      "height": "96vh"
    },
    {
      "id": "b-26f00i",
      "type": "text",
      "lead": true,
      "dropcap": true,
      "wide": false,
      "body": "Is it the jinx of 26? The catastrophic flash floods that tore through Nepal on August 26 are the latest addition to a grim history of disasters that struck on the 26th — right from the 1939 earthquake in Turkey that killed more than 32,700 people to the 2004 Boxing Day tsunami in which 228,000 people were killed, missing and presumed dead.\n\nAnd it is not just about natural disasters. The date is also when the Mumbai terror siege began on the night of November 26, 2008, and of the Chernobyl nuclear accident on April 26, 1986."
    },
