        {
          "id": "s-26f01v",
          "visual": "v-26f01q",
          "number": "",
          "body": "### The 1930s and 1940s\n\nFive entries in fourteen years, at both ends of the scale: a Greek village, two Turkish earthquakes, a Japanese peninsula, and a coal mine in Liaoning.",
          "align": "left",
          "state": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "s-26f01w",
          "visual": "v-26f01r",
          "number": "",
          "body": "### 1963 to 1991\n\nThen a long, quiet gap. Skopje stands almost alone for two decades before Chernobyl, and the entries that follow are smaller.",
          "align": "left",
          "state": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "s-26f01x",
          "visual": "v-26f01s",
          "number": "",
          "body": "### 2001 to 2008\n\nSeven disasters in eight years — the densest stretch on the record, and the one that contains the tsunami.",
          "align": "left",
          "state": {}
        },
        {
          "id": "s-26f01y",
          "visual": "v-26f01t",
          "number": "",
          "body": "### 2015 to 2026\n\nAnd the recent end: the Hindu Kush, Kabul, a wedding hall near Mosul, and now Nepal.",
          "align": "left",
          "state": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-26f01z",
      "type": "text",
      "lead": false,
      "dropcap": false,
      "wide": false,
      "body": "## The rest of the record\n\nSeven more natural disasters fell on a 26th. They are smaller in the count only.\n\n### Hindu Kush earthquake — Afghanistan / Pakistan\n\n*26 October 2015.* A magnitude 7.5 earthquake struck the Hindu Kush region, affecting Afghanistan, Pakistan and parts of northern India. It reportedly killed about **400 people**, most of them in Pakistan and Afghanistan.\n\n### Afghanistan earthquakes — Baghlan, Afghanistan\n\n*26 March 2002.* A series of earthquakes struck northern Afghanistan overnight on March 25–26, devastating parts of Baghlan province. Mud-brick homes collapsed and thousands were left homeless. Early estimates put the toll as high as **1,800**, though later assessments varied significantly.\n\n### Gonghe earthquake — Qinghai, China\n\n*26 April 1990.* A magnitude 7 earthquake struck Qinghai province, causing severe damage in the Gonghe region and killing **more than 100 people**.\n\n### Pantar earthquake and tsunami — Pantar, Indonesia\n\n*26 November 1987.* A magnitude 6.5 earthquake in the Pantar area of Indonesia generated a tsunami. The disaster killed **125 people**.\n\n### Sea of Japan earthquake — Akita and Aomori, Japan\n\n*26 May 1983.* A magnitude 7.8 earthquake off northern Japan triggered a tsunami that hit coastal Akita and Aomori. The wave caused most of the casualties: **104 killed**.\n\n### Lerissos earthquake — Chalkidiki, Greece\n\n*26 September 1932.* A powerful earthquake struck the Chalkidiki peninsula, severely damaging Lerissos. Hundreds of houses were destroyed and a tsunami was recorded. Historical estimates of the toll range from **about 160 to nearly 500**.\n\n### North Izu earthquake — Izu peninsula, Japan\n\n*26 November 1930.* A magnitude 7.3 earthquake struck Japan’s Izu peninsula, with landslides and fires adding to the destruction. **Around 250 people** were killed; later catalogues record 272."
    },
    {
      "id": "b-26f020",
      "type": "quote",
      "style": "center",
      "text": "About 228,000 people were killed or left missing and presumed dead — one of the deadliest natural disasters in recorded history.",
      "cite": "On the Indian Ocean tsunami, 26 December 2004"
    },
    {
      "id": "b-26f021",
      "type": "text",
      "lead": false,
      "dropcap": false,
      "wide": false,
      "body": "## A note on the date\n\nDates recur. With thirty-one of them and a century of record-keeping, some number was always going to collect a heavy share of catastrophe, and the 26th has collected this one.\n\nWhat the list documents is not a property of the date. It is the ordinary arithmetic of a violent century, seen through a single square on the calendar — and, in each of these twenty-two entries, a great many people who did not come home."
    }
